{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_011Ch3nhERp5GdHqLgHCgUdJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"I haven't made this change yet, because a single commit would keep the fix from reaching the import for two of the three months.\n\n**The problem:** the README says the nightly import reloads only the month that a commit message starts with. A commit that touches several months loads only that one. If I put all three fixes in one commit, say `2025-10: fix wholsale`, then November and December keep `wholsale` downstream even though the repo looks fixed.\n\n**The scope:** one bad row in each month (Oct, Nov, Dec). No other month and nothing in `inbox/` has the typo.\n\n**I'd suggest three commits,** `2025-10: fix wholesale spelling`, then the same for `2025-11` and `2025-12`, made back to back. You can still undo all three at once:\n\n```\ngit revert --no-commit HEAD~3..HEAD && git commit -m \"...\"\n```\n\nOne catch: undoing the fix runs into the same import rule. Each month's revert would also need its own commit for the import to reload that month.\n\nShould I go with three commits, or do you still want one? A single commit might be fine if the import has some other way to pick up all three months that the README doesn't mention.","citations":[]}],"model":"anthropic/claude-opus-5.5","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":394,"cache_read_input_tokens":22636,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":394,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":"standard","speed":"standard"},"provider":"Anthropic","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<uuid-1>","uuid":"<uuid-69>","timestamp":"2026-11-15T00:44:49.982Z","request_id":"gen-1794703484-XwUxQ1cUDmCqyh6sQbjC"}
